3. Functional Requirements (FR)

3.1 Offline Evaluation Engine (FR-1)

FR-1.1 Critique Shadowing Orchestrator: The system shall provide an interface for Domain Experts to review system traces, submit binary classifications (Pass/Fail), and record natural language justifications.

FR-1.2 Judge Bootstrap Engine: The system shall ingest expert critiques and use them to auto-generate few-shot prompt templates for LLM-as-a-Judge agents.

FR-1.3 Evaluator Discrepancy Matrix: The system shall compute inter-annotator agreement metrics (e.g., Cohen's Kappa) between human experts and LLM-as-a-Judge agents, highlighting divergence clusters for prompt revision.

FR-1.4 Micro-Rubric Slicer: Rather than holistic scoring, the system shall segment evaluation tasks into targeted, modular criteria (e.g., "hallucination check," "brand compliance," "instruction adherence") and execute them across specialized, small-parameter judge models.

3.2 Online Runtime Guardrails (FR-2)

FR-2.1 Bidirectional Guardrail Pipeline: The system shall intercept incoming payloads (Pre-LLM) and outgoing generations (Post-LLM) in real time.

FR-2.2 Multi-Tier Engine Support: The system shall support both rules-based, deterministic scanners (Regex, structural validation) and heuristic semantic engines (embedding-based vector classification, Llama Guard/Guardrails AI).

FR-2.3 Reactive Self-Correction Loop: When a post-LLM guardrail triggers a validation failure, the system shall programmatically halt output delivery, bundle the raw draft, append the specific validation violation text, and route a correction payload back to the generator LLM.

FR-2.4 Stacking Optimization Module: The system shall provide a visual calculator and optimizer to monitor and mitigate "guardrail stacking decay" (preventing compound false-positives from locking down benign user prompts).

3.3 Security & Pipeline Validation (FR-3)

FR-3.1 Automated Red Teaming Execution: The system shall run scheduled, agentic adversarial simulations against production endpoints to identify boundary exploits, prompt injections, and privilege-escalation vectors.

FR-3.2 Ingestion & ETL Data Auditing: The system shall monitor RAG ingestion pipelines, verifying source document compliance, licensing parameters, and running automated data-lineage scans prior to chunking and vector storage.

3.4 Regulatory Gating & Compliance Generator (FR-4)

FR-4.1 Technical Documentation Assembler (Annex IV): The system shall automatically aggregate system version logs, data-profiling metrics, human-in-the-loop audit logs, and red-teaming reports to generate compliant Technical Files matching Article 11 / Annex IV of the EU AI Act.

FR-4.2 Automated Compliance Mapping: The system shall map pipeline validations directly to ISO/IEC 42001 clauses and NIST AI RMF functions, flagging missing controls or gaps in the continuous integration pipelines.

4. Technical Specifications & Mathematical Controls

4.1 Stacking Reliability Mathematics

To prevent high false-positive rates when utilizing multiple independent guardrails, the system must enforce strict operational constraints based on the compound probability of false positives.

Let $k$ be the number of independent runtime guardrails, and let $P(FP_i)$ be the probability of a false positive for the $i$-th guardrail. The aggregate false-positive rate $P(FP_{\text{total}})$ is defined mathematically as:

$$P(FP_{\text{total}}) = 1 - \prod_{i=1}^{k} (1 - P(FP_i))$$

Operational Constraint: Aegis-Val's optimizer shall flag any guardrail stack where the estimated compound $P(FP_{\text{total}}) > 0.15$. The system will automatically recommend moving high-latency, higher-FP evaluations to asynchronous, non-blocking post-execution tracing, or suggest substituting a heuristic check with a deterministic, low-error check.

4.2 API Specifications: Critique Shadowing Pipeline

The system shall expose the following JSON schema endpoints for managing the human-to-judge loop:

Endpoint: POST /api/v1/critique-shadowing/submit

